1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,469
Five blocks, one frame, and a tree that says where each block goes.

2
00:00:04,469 --> 00:00:07,537
Can you build the picture from the tree alone?

3
00:00:07,537 --> 00:00:12,072
By the end, you will, and you will find the tightest picture of all.

4
00:00:12,072 --> 00:00:16,184
A chip is made of large rectangular parts, called blocks.

5
00:00:16,184 --> 00:00:18,709
Ours has five blocks, named A to E.

6
00:00:18,709 --> 00:00:23,904
They are drawn on squared paper, so you can count each width and height.

7
00:00:23,904 --> 00:00:27,396
A floorplan says where each block sits on the chip.

8
00:00:27,396 --> 00:00:31,231
Every block must stay inside a frame called the outline.

9
00:00:31,231 --> 00:00:34,998
Our outline is ten squares wide and eight squares tall.

10
00:00:34,998 --> 00:00:39,312
The tighter the blocks are packed, the smaller the chip can be.

11
00:00:39,312 --> 00:00:45,284
Writing down every position is slow, so we use a recipe instead: a B-star tree.

12
00:00:45,284 --> 00:00:47,929
Each block is one node of the tree.

13
00:00:47,929 --> 00:00:50,650
The top node, A, is called the root.

14
00:00:50,650 --> 00:00:55,488
Each node can have two children: a left child and a right child.

15
00:00:55,488 --> 00:00:59,100
The root goes in the lower-left corner of the outline.

16
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:03,984
From that corner, x counts squares to the right, and y counts squares up.

17
00:01:03,984 --> 00:01:08,760
A left child goes next door, right against its parent's right side.

18
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:14,106
A right child goes upstairs, on top of its parent, with the same left edge.

19
00:01:14,106 --> 00:01:20,023
The names sound backwards, so remember: left means next door, right means upstairs.

20
00:01:20,023 --> 00:01:25,440
In the tree, a solid line means next door, and a dashed line means upstairs.

21
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:29,171
A is three squares wide and two squares tall.

22
00:01:29,171 --> 00:01:30,829
B is A's left child.

23
00:01:30,829 --> 00:01:34,560
Pause and guess: where does B's left edge go?

24
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:40,443
B goes next door, at x equals three: A starts at zero, and A is three wide.

25
00:01:40,443 --> 00:01:45,384
Nothing is under B, so it rests on the floor, at y equals zero.

26
00:01:45,384 --> 00:01:49,731
We place the blocks one at a time, in a fixed walk order.

27
00:01:49,731 --> 00:01:55,831
Place a node, then everything down its left side, and only then its right child.

28
00:01:55,831 --> 00:01:59,568
So the walk goes A, B, C and E, and D comes last.

29
00:01:59,568 --> 00:02:04,874
C is B's left child, so it goes next door to B, at x equals five.

30
00:02:04,874 --> 00:02:10,342
E is C's left child, so it lands next door to C, at x equals seven.

31
00:02:10,342 --> 00:02:15,648
The widths simply add up: three, plus two, plus two, makes seven.

32
00:02:15,648 --> 00:02:21,258
The left side is done, so the walk comes back to D, A's right child.

33
00:02:21,258 --> 00:02:25,795
D goes upstairs, so it starts at x equals zero, like A.

34
00:02:25,795 --> 00:02:28,848
It sits on top of A, at y equals two.

35
00:02:28,848 --> 00:02:33,383
All five blocks are placed, and every one is inside the outline.

36
00:02:33,383 --> 00:02:39,264
Pause and guess: if we swap A and C in the tree, does D still land at y equals two?

37
00:02:39,264 --> 00:02:41,736
No: D lands one square higher.

38
00:02:41,736 --> 00:02:48,327
Here is the tree with A and C swapped: C is the root, and A takes C's old place.

39
00:02:48,327 --> 00:02:52,776
C, B, A and E still go next door, one after the other.

40
00:02:52,776 --> 00:02:56,479
Now picture the blocks as a city, seen from far away.

41
00:02:56,479 --> 00:03:00,391
The top edge of everything placed so far is the skyline.

42
00:03:00,391 --> 00:03:06,120
A new block drops from the sky, like a Tetris piece, until it touches the skyline.

43
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:10,123
D is three wide, so it hangs over C and over part of B.

44
00:03:10,123 --> 00:03:13,690
B is three tall, so it stops D at y equals three.

45
00:03:13,690 --> 00:03:17,184
That leaves a hole of two empty squares above C.

46
00:03:17,184 --> 00:03:19,325
So which floorplan is better?

47
00:03:19,325 --> 00:03:25,451
To compare, we draw the bounding box: the smallest rectangle around all the blocks.

48
00:03:25,451 --> 00:03:30,840
For the first tree, it is nine wide and three tall: twenty-seven squares.

49
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:33,982
The five blocks cover twenty-three squares.

50
00:03:33,982 --> 00:03:38,805
The other four squares in the box are empty: that is wasted space.

51
00:03:38,805 --> 00:03:42,240
Fewer wasted squares means a tighter floorplan.

52
00:03:42,240 --> 00:03:48,197
The swapped tree needs a box nine by four: thirty-six squares, with thirteen wasted.

53
00:03:48,197 --> 00:03:52,382
So one swap of two names changed the picture and the score.

54
00:03:52,382 --> 00:03:56,424
Twenty-seven became thirty-six: the first tree is better.

55
00:03:56,424 --> 00:03:59,969
Now take a tree where every child is a left child.

56
00:03:59,969 --> 00:04:04,152
Pause and guess: will this long row fit inside the outline?

57
00:04:04,152 --> 00:04:05,884
No, it will not fit.

58
00:04:05,884 --> 00:04:11,338
Every block goes next door: three, two, two and three make ten.

59
00:04:11,338 --> 00:04:15,840
The outline is already full, and E is still waiting.

60
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:20,309
E lands at x equals ten, so the row is twelve squares wide.

61
00:04:20,309 --> 00:04:23,566
E sticks out of the outline by two squares.

62
00:04:23,566 --> 00:04:28,490
A floorplan is legal only when every block is inside the outline.

63
00:04:28,490 --> 00:04:30,384
So this row is not legal.

64
00:04:30,384 --> 00:04:33,032
Notice that the blocks never overlap.

65
00:04:33,032 --> 00:04:38,400
Each block drops onto the skyline, so it can never land inside another one.

66
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:42,480
With a B-star tree, sticking out is the only way to fail.

67
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:47,054
We can turn a block on its side, so its width and height swap.

68
00:04:47,054 --> 00:04:50,521
Turn D, and it becomes one wide and three tall.

69
00:04:50,521 --> 00:04:55,464
Now the row is ten wide, so it fits exactly, and it is legal again.

70
00:04:55,464 --> 00:04:58,188
Turning is one of three small changes.

71
00:04:58,188 --> 00:05:02,776
You can also swap two names in the tree, as we did with A and C.

72
00:05:02,776 --> 00:05:07,723
Or move a leaf, a block with no children, to a free spot in the tree.

73
00:05:07,723 --> 00:05:11,450
Each change gives a new floorplan, with a new score.

74
00:05:11,450 --> 00:05:17,257
Pause and guess: can any tree beat twenty-seven squares, and what is the smallest

75
00:05:17,257 --> 00:05:17,544
box?

76
00:05:17,544 --> 00:05:24,084
Yes: many trees beat twenty-seven, and the best need only twenty-four squares.

77
00:05:24,084 --> 00:05:26,599
Here is one of the best trees.

78
00:05:26,599 --> 00:05:30,624
Its walk places A, D, C and E, and B comes last.

79
00:05:30,624 --> 00:05:35,815
Under B, the skyline is the top of D, so B lands at y equals one.

80
00:05:35,815 --> 00:05:41,964
It fills the gap, and the box is six wide and four tall: twenty-four squares.

81
00:05:41,964 --> 00:05:44,040
Only one square is wasted.

82
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:49,415
Could a box of twenty-three squares, with nothing wasted, hold all five blocks?

83
00:05:49,415 --> 00:05:53,497
Twenty-three squares only make a rectangle one square thick.

84
00:05:53,497 --> 00:05:58,056
Block B is too thick for that, so twenty-four is the best possible.

85
00:05:58,056 --> 00:06:00,189
Now build floorplans yourself.

86
00:06:00,189 --> 00:06:05,947
In the browser lab, you edit the tree and watch each block drop onto the skyline.

87
00:06:05,947 --> 00:06:08,791
The challenges check the tree you built.

88
00:06:08,791 --> 00:06:13,056
Lift D higher, make the long row fit, and reach twenty-four.

89
00:06:13,056 --> 00:06:16,539
If you like programming, write the packer in Python.

90
00:06:16,539 --> 00:06:19,217
The examples file gives the exact rules.

91
00:06:19,217 --> 00:06:24,642
Run the checker once to print every correct answer, then run it on your own file.

92
00:06:24,642 --> 00:06:30,000
It compares your packer with the right one on all five thousand and forty trees.

93
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:31,684
Left child: next door.

94
00:06:31,684 --> 00:06:35,280
Right child: upstairs, with the same left edge.

95
00:06:35,280 --> 00:06:40,408
Walk the tree: the node, its whole left side, then its right child.

96
00:06:40,408 --> 00:06:43,928
Each block drops until it touches the skyline.

97
00:06:43,928 --> 00:06:49,285
Score a floorplan with its bounding box, and count the wasted squares.

98
00:06:49,285 --> 00:06:54,336
A tree never overlaps, but it can stick out, so check the outline.

99
00:06:54,336 --> 00:06:57,940
Pick a track: the browser lab, Python, or both.

100
00:06:57,940 --> 00:07:02,311
Tick each box of the checklist, then take the short quiz.

101
00:07:02,311 --> 00:07:07,448
Next comes the sequence pair: two lists of names instead of a tree.

102
00:07:07,448 --> 00:07:13,276
Pause and guess: can two lists describe our best floorplan, with twenty-four

103
00:07:13,276 --> 00:07:13,890
squares?

104
00:07:13,890 --> 00:07:15,883
Yes: two lists are enough.

105
00:07:15,883 --> 00:07:19,104
That's the sequence pair, in the next lab.
